How to run the integration tests
================================

There are 4 main steps do run Torizon OS integration tests:

1. Configure the development machine
2. Download Torizon OS images
3. Install a fresh Torizon OS image in a device (optional)
4. Run the tests

Steps 1, 2 and 4 can be executed automatically using the run_all.sh wrapper script.

Quickstart using run_all.sh
===========================

To run all integration tests for a Toradex Easy Installer image for a specific machine
(e.g. verdin-imx8mp) using the latest release of TCB, run:

$ ./run_all.sh --machine verdin-imx8mp

Set IS_WIC=1 when running tests for a WIC raw image:

$ IS_WIC=1 ./run_all.sh --machine intel-corei7-64

Run:

$ ./run_all.sh --help

for additional options like run specific testcases, run device tests, or test a specific TCB image.

The rest of this README goes in-depth for each of the 4 steps mentioned above.

1. Configure the machine
========================

First step is to install some dependencies (you may need to adapt this
command to other Linux distributions):

$ sudo apt install sshpass zstd avahi-utils openssl wget bash tar zip bc bats git

And then source the setup script:

$ source setup.sh

By default setup.sh will download tcb-env-setup.sh from the Toradex GitHub repo and
run it with the '-c -a remote' arguments.

You can pass additional arguments to setup.sh, which will then be forwarded to tcb-env-setup.sh,
replacing the defaults (-a remote is still added if neither -t nor -a is given).

setup.sh can be configured with the following environment variables:

TCB_SCRIPT_BRANCH:    Override the default tcb-env-setup.sh Git branch/commit from https://github.com/toradex/tcb-env-setup.
                      The value can be a branch name or a SHA commit.
TCB_SKIP_SCRIPT_PULL: Set to 1 to skip the tcb-env-setup.sh download and use the copy
                      already at workdir/tcb-env-setup.sh.
                      *NOTE: This replaces TCB_SKIP_PULL from older versions of setup.sh,
                      which also skipped pulling the TCB image. To get the old behaviour,
                      also set TCB_NO_PULL=1.*
TCB_NO_PULL:          Used by tcb-env-setup.sh. Set it to 1 to skip any attempts to pull the TCB image remotely.

The tcb-env-setup.sh script can also be configured using other environment variables and additional arguments. Check
https://github.com/toradex/tcb-env-setup for more details.

2. Option 1: Download Torizon OS Easy Installer (TEZI) images
=============================================================

You can manually download Torizon OS Tezi images from Toradex's
Artifactory and save them on $PWD/workdir/images. In this case, the
images must be in tarball format, as downloaded from Artifactory. Also,
it is necessary to create a stamp file in the images directory so the
test scripts know the images were downloaded:

$ touch $PWD/workdir/images/.images_downloaded

Another option is to use get_tezi_images.sh script to automatically
download the images based on a specific Torizon OS version.

To use the script, you can run the script with the default values:

$ ./get_tezi_images.sh

If you want to change build type, machine or the yocto branch please set 
the respectively enviroment variables, an example would be:

$ TARGET_BUILD_TYPE=release YOCTO_BRANCH=kirkstone-6.x.y TCB_MACHINE=verdin-am62 ./get_tezi_images.sh

2. Option 2: Download Common Torizon OS WIC/raw images
======================================================

You can manually download Torizon OS WIC/raw images from the Common Torizon
GitHub repository:

https://github.com/commontorizon/meta-common-torizon/releases

The images should be saved in $PWD/workdir/images, and similarly to the
TEZI instructions a stamp file must be created to indicate that the
images were downloaded.

$ touch $PWD/workdir/images/.raw_images_downloaded

There's also the option to use get_raw_images.sh to automatically
download specific Common Torizon versions according to user-defined
parameters. To have this please set the enviroment variable TCVERSION:

$ TCVERSION=6.4.0-common ./get_raw_images.sh

3. Install a fresh Torizon OS image in a device
===============================================

In case you also want to run test cases that requires device access,
install a fresh Torizon OS image in the device and set the default
password to '1' before running the tests.

4. Run the tests
================

There is a script called run.sh that will run the integration tests
for you.

The script is configurable via environment variables, and the
following variables are available:

TCB_DEVICE:       IP address of the device used in test cases that
                  requires device access.
TCB_PORT:         SSH port of the device used in test cases that
                  requires device access (default port: 22)
TCB_TESTCASE:     Test case to be executed, in case you want to run a
                  specific group of tests. If you want to run multiple
                  groups of tests, use a string delimited by white spaces:
                  "dt dto"
TCB_MACHINE:      The test scripts need a Torizon OS image to run the
                  test cases (apalis-imx6 image is the default). With
                  this variable, you can select which image will be used
                  in the test cases, based on the defined MACHINE.
TCB_REPORT:       If set to '1', it will enable the generation of a test
                  report in $PWD/workdir/reports.
TCB_UNDER_CI:     If set to '1', some tests not appropriate for running
                  under CI will be skipped.
TCB_TAGS:         Only run tests that match all the tags in the list (&&).
                  Separating the lists by space allows for logical or (||):
                  `a,b x,y` matches tags (a && b) || (x && y)
                  A '!' before a tag negates it: `!secboot` matches tests
                  without the 'secboot' tag.
TCB_K3_REFERENCE_KEY:
                  Path to the key that signed the image under test, which
                  enables the secboot cases that compare a re-signed
                  bootloader binary with the original one byte for byte.
                  Only a re-sign with the same key can reproduce those
                  bytes, so without it those cases skip. The path is
                  resolved inside the container, where the test working
                  directory is mounted at /workdir, so give it relative to
                  that directory rather than as a path on the host.
                  On an AM62 image the GP boot containers are signed with
                  a degenerate key, and the re-sign uses the one
                  TorizonCore Builder ships. For an image built with
                  another degenerate key those bytes differ and the
                  comparison fails, so leave this variable unset for it.
TCB_REQUIRE_SIGNED_IMAGE:
                  If set to '1', stop with an error before running any
                  test when the image under test has no signing
                  artifacts, instead of skipping every case that needs a
                  signed image.

Following are some examples of running the test cases.

The command below will run all test cases that doesn't need a device,
using a Apalis iMX6 image:

$ ./run.sh

The command below will run all test cases that doesn't need a device,
using a Verdin iMX8MM image:

$ TCB_MACHINE=verdin-imx8mm ./run.sh

The command below will execute all test cases, including those that
requires device access (the image used in the tests will be
automatically selected based on the device):

$ TCB_DEVICE=192.168.1.210 TCB_MACHINE=apalis-imx6 DEVICE_PASSWORD=<password> ./run.sh

The command below will execute the test cases defined in
testcases/dto.bats, using an Apalis iMX6 image, and a test report
will be available in workdir/reports/:

$ TCB_REPORT=1 TCB_TESTCASE=dto TCB_MACHINE=apalis-imx6 ./run.sh

The command below will execute the test cases defined in both
testcases/dt.bats and testcases/dto.bats:

$ TCB_TESTCASE="dt dto" TCB_MACHINE=apalis-imx6 ./run.sh

The command below will execute all test cases, including those that
require device access:

$ TCB_DEVICE=192.168.1.210 TCB_MACHINE=apalis-imx6 DEVICE_PASSWORD=<password> ./run.sh

The command below will execute all the tests:
$ TCB_TAGS="all" TCB_MACHINE=apalis-imx6 ./run.sh

The command below will execute untagged tests:
$ TCB_TAGS="" TCB_MACHINE=apalis-imx6 ./run.sh

The command below will execute the secure boot tests, which are tagged as
'secboot' and so are left out of a run of untagged tests:
$ TCB_TAGS="secboot" TCB_TESTCASE="secboot build" TCB_MACHINE=verdin-am62 ./run.sh

The command below will execute tests tagged as 'requires-device':
$ TCB_TAGS="requires-device" TCB_DEVICE=192.168.1.210 TCB_MACHINE=apalis-imx6 DEVICE_PASSWORD=<password> ./run.sh

Running the tests on MAGMA (CI)
===============================

The hardware-in-the-loop CI jobs (magma/magma-tests.yml) flash each
board with `magma-client flash --tos-password`, which writes a
shell-sourceable device information file:

export DEVICE_IP="10.200.105.43"
export DEVICE_HOSTNAME="colibri-imx6ull-emmc-06906475"

run_all.sh reads it with `--device-info <file>` (default:
device_information). The jobs need the LAB_SSH_PRIVATE_KEY (the lab
user's private key, base64-encoded on a single line) and
DEVICE_PASSWORD CI variables.
